Hello Andrei,

I would like to propose the following use case for my final project:

*Project Topic: Secure & Explainable AI-Powered SOC*

The goal of the project is to develop a lightweight AI-assisted SOC
prototype that detects suspicious activity from security telemetry and
transforms the detection into an interpretable and actionable alert for a
SOC analyst.

The proposed pipeline will include:

   -

   *Anomaly Detection* =E2=80=93 using machine learning models such as Isol=
ation
   Forest and Autoencoder to identify suspicious behavior.
   -

   *Explainable AI (XAI)* =E2=80=93 providing evidence and feature-level
   explanations for why an event was classified as anomalous.
   -

   *MITRE ATT&CK Mapping* =E2=80=93 mapping detected behavior to relevant M=
ITRE
   ATT&CK tactics and techniques.
   -

   *Local LLM (Llama)* =E2=80=93 generating a structured, human-readable SO=
C
   analysis based on the detection evidence, XAI results, and MITRE mapping=
.
   -

   *Responsible AI / Human-in-the-Loop* =E2=80=93 keeping the AI in an advi=
sory
   role while the final security decision remains with the human analyst.

In addition, I would like to evaluate the *security of the AI-assisted SOC
itself*. I plan to test whether adversarial context, such as
direct/indirect prompt injection or authority manipulation, can influence
the LLM's recommendation while the underlying security telemetry remains
unchanged.

I will then evaluate an evidence-based defense architecture using trust
separation, evidence validation, and RAI controls, and compare the system's
behavior *before and after these defenses are applied*.

The main objective is therefore to examine both sides of AI in
cybersecurity: using AI to support threat detection and SOC analysis, while
also treating the AI component itself as part of the attack surface that
must be secured.

The initial implementation will be designed as a *SOC Lite environment*
that can run locally using Llama without requiring a heavy LLM. If
appropriate and feasible, NVIDIA Morpheus/accelerated processing can later
be incorporated as an extension to the pipeline.

The project will evaluate detection performance, explainability, AI attack
success rates, and the effectiveness of the proposed defense mechanisms.

I would appreciate your feedback and approval regarding this project
direction.

Best regards,
Aviv Rom Horesh

